1. Problem Breakdown (Simple Summary)
Goal: Create a performance summary report for each ad campaign.
Filter rule: Only include campaigns that have more than 15 ad views (impressions > 15).
Columns needed:
Campaign name (ad_campaign)
Total views (impressions)
Total money earned (total_revenue)
Click-Through Rate as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place (ctr)
Sorting rule: Highest ctr first. If two campaigns tie on ctr, sort them alphabetically (A → Z) by ad_campaign.

2. The Intuition (How to Think About It)
Imagine you have a giant box of 100 million paper receipts, and each receipt represents one time someone saw an ad.
Sort receipts into folders (GROUP BY): Since we want numbers per campaign, create one folder for each ad_campaign and drop its receipts inside.
Toss out small folders (HAVING): Count the receipts in each folder. If a folder has 15 or fewer receipts, throw it in the trash. 
(Interview tip: We use HAVING instead of WHERE because we are filtering folders after counting them, not individual receipts).
Do the math for each surviving folder (SELECT):
Impressions: Count total receipts in the folder (COUNT(*)).
Revenue: Add up the revenue written on those receipts (SUM(revenue)).
CTR %: Count only the receipts marked clicked = TRUE (turn TRUE into 1 and FALSE into 0, then SUM them), divide by total receipts in the folder, multiply by 100.0 (use .0 so SQL doesn't chop off decimals!), and round to 1 decimal place.
Arrange the final report (ORDER BY): Put the highest ctr at the top (DESC), and use ad_campaign ASC to break ties.

SELECT 
    ad_campaign,
    COUNT(*) AS impressions,
    SUM(revenue) AS total_revenue,
    ROUND(100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN clicked THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / COUNT(*), 1) AS ctr
FROM ad_impressions
GROUP BY ad_campaign
HAVING COUNT(*) > 15
ORDER BY ctr DESC, ad_campaign ASC;

